@page "/group-rides/{RideId:guid}"
@attribute [Authorize]
@using BlazorDLR.Shared.Components
@using BlazorDLR.Shared.Markers
@using DLR.Core.Contracts.Markers
@using DLR.Core.Contracts.Rides
@using DLR.Core.Tracks
@inject IApiClient Api
@inject IRideHubClient Hub
@inject AuthState Auth
@inject ConfirmService Confirm
@inject CurrentRideState CurrentRide
@inject IServiceProvider Services
@inject NavigationManager Nav
@inject RideSnapshotCache Cache
@inject RouteStyleState RouteStyles
@inject IScreenWakeLock ScreenWakeLock
@inject IDeviceSettings Settings
@inject TimeProvider Clock
@implements IAsyncDisposable

@*
	The live ride (§5.3). The map IS the page: it fills everything the nav rail is not
	using, and every affordance but the two camera modes is reached from the hamburger in
	the top-right.

	Those two — "follow me" and "heading up" — are buttons in a stack under the hamburger and
	live nowhere else. They are the only things on this screen a rider does while actually
	moving, and everything the menu holds is done at a stop, so they are the ones that have
	earned a permanent target rather than a tap, a read and a second tap. They are also the
	only two that outlive the tap that set them, and a lit button is what says so — which is
	why the hamburger no longer carries a dot for either of them. Choosing heading up switches
	following on too: a map that turns about a point the rider is not standing on is worse than
	a north-up one.

	Why a hamburger here and nowhere else. NavMenu.razor states the app-wide rule — the
	rail never collapses behind a toggle, because hiding *navigation* is hiding the app.
	This menu hides no destination: the rail is still on screen next to it. It holds this
	one screen's *actions*, which is what a screen with no room for a toolbar does.

	Everything that reads rather than acts — who is on the ride, the gaps, the join code,
	the sharing switch, the organiser's controls — lives on GroupRideInfo, one tap away
	under "Info". A rider looking at a live map wants the map.
*@

<PageTitle>@Title — Dumb Luck Routes</PageTitle>

<div class="page-full live">
	@if (_session.Ride is null && _session.Error is null)
	{
		<p class="empty">Loading…</p>
	}
	else if (_session.Ride is not { } ride)
	{
		<p class="error">@_session.Error</p>
	}
	else
	{
		<div class="map-fill">
			@* ShowPickingCursor follows the mode rather than being off outright: an ordinary tap
			   here interrogates what is already on the map, but a tap while placing does put
			   something down, and that is the one time a crosshair is honest. *@
			@*
				ShowUserLocation is the base map's own blue dot, and it is asked for only on a host
				that has no receiver of its own — the browser (§18.6). It cannot work on the phone:
				MapLibre's GeolocateControl reads navigator.geolocation, which is the *WebView's*
				permission gate and not the one the app holds for ILocationProvider — MAUI's
				BlazorWebView grants it on neither platform, and it never resolves or errors. It
				would also only start on a tap of its own button, which nothing here performs.

				The phones draw themselves on the Skia overlay instead, from this device's own fix
				— see SelfMarker. Note that the browser's dot answers a different question from the
				rider pill beside it: where the machine reading the map is, which for somebody
				watching a ride from a laptop is not where their bike is.
			*@
			<RideMap @ref="_map" Camera="_camera" Markers="_markerLayer" Routes="_routeLayer"
					 Circles="_privateAreaLayer" ShowUserLocation="!HasOwnReceiver"
					 OnViewportReported="OnMapViewportReportedAsync"
					 OnGesture="OnMapGestureAsync"
					 OnMapClicked="OnMapTappedAsync" ShowPickingCursor="_placingMarker" />
		</div>

		@*
			The receiver needs the rider to do something — grant permission, or read why fixes are
			not landing (§4.3). It earns a strip over the map only when both halves are true: the
			rider has asked to share, and the state is one they can act on. A rider who is not
			sharing is not owed a GPS message at all.

			Above the map's own error strip and styled the same way, because to a rider they are
			the same kind of thing: something the app could not do, said out loud rather than left
			to be inferred from a pin that never moves.
		*@
		@*
			The ride could not be fetched and this device's own copy was drawn instead (§4.4).

			It has to be said out loud and it has to carry an age. A map of where the group was
			twenty minutes ago is the most useful thing a rider who has lost signal can be handed
			and the most dangerous thing to mistake for live — riding towards a pin that stopped
			moving before the group turned off is exactly the failure — and the only difference
			between those two readings is whether the app said which one it was.

			First in the chain, ahead of the receiver's own complaint. Both are true at once out
			here — a phone that cannot fetch a ride cannot publish a fix either — and of the two,
			"you are looking at an old copy" is the one that changes what the rider does with what
			is on screen. The receiver's state is not lost by yielding the strip: it is in the menu
			under the hamburger, on every frame, which is where a rider goes to ask.
		*@
		@if (_session.LoadedFromCache)
		{
			<div class="map-error cached" role="status">
				<i class="fa fa-cloud-arrow-down" aria-hidden="true"></i>
				<span>Offline — this adventure as it was @CachedAge.</span>
			</div>
		}
		else if (_session.Sharing && _broadcast is { NeedsAttention: true } alerting)
		{
			<div class="map-error gps-alert error" role="alert">
				<i class="fa fa-location-crosshairs" aria-hidden="true"></i>
				<span>@alerting.Describe()</span>
			</div>
		}
		else if (_session.SharingPending)
		{
			@*
				Sharing is on and the ride has not started, so nothing this device sends would be
				kept (§5.1) — and the receiver is not running, so there is nothing to report about
				it either. Said out loud rather than left as an empty map: the rider has agreed to
				share, been through the disclosure and the phone's permission prompts, and is owed
				the reason they are still the only thing not on screen.

				Not styled as an error. Nothing has gone wrong; the ride simply has not started.
			*@
			<div class="map-error gps-alert" role="status">
				<i class="fa fa-location-crosshairs" aria-hidden="true"></i>
				<span>Sharing is on. Your position starts going to the group when the organiser starts the adventure.</span>
			</div>
		}

		@* An error raised by something the menu did — the map underneath is still usable, so
		   this is a dismissible strip rather than a page that replaced itself with a message. *@
		@if (_session.Error is { } problem)
		{
			<div class="map-error error" role="alert">
				<span>@problem</span>
				<button type="button" class="close" @onclick="_session.ClearError" aria-label="Dismiss">×</button>
			</div>
		}

		@*
			The one mode this screen has: the next tap on the map is a marker's point (§16.1).

			It has to be visible, because an armed map behaves differently from the map the
			rider was just using — a tap on a pin stops opening its detail — and a mode nobody
			can see is a screen that has quietly stopped answering. It carries its own way out
			for the same reason: the alternative is placing a marker you no longer want just to
			get the map back.
		*@
		@if (_placingMarker)
		{
			<div class="placing" role="status">
				<span>Tap the map where the marker goes.</span>
				<button type="button" @onclick="CancelPlacingMarker">Cancel</button>
			</div>
		}

		@*
			What the map just started or stopped doing on its own, said for a few seconds and
			then gone (§5.3).

			The two automatic modes are the only things on this screen that change what the map
			does *after* the tap that set them, and one of them — following — can also be
			cancelled by a gesture the rider made for another reason entirely. Both cases are the
			same problem: a map that has quietly changed its mind is indistinguishable from a map
			that is broken. So each transition says so once, in the rider's own words, and then
			stops taking up the screen.

			Transient rather than a strip that stays, because the standing statement of both modes
			is already on screen permanently — each has a button in the stack that lights up when
			it is on. This is for the *change*.

			aria-live="polite" rather than assertive: nothing here interrupts anything, and a rider
			with a screen reader riding a bike is the last person to interrupt mid-sentence.
			`raised` lifts it clear of the placing strip on the rare frame that has both.

			**`mode-toast`, and never `toast`.** Bootstrap is linked by all three hosts and defines
			`.toast:not(.show){display:none}` — its own toast is a JS component that is invisible
			until its script adds `.show`. A plain `toast` class here is therefore not a styling
			clash that looks a bit wrong, it is an element that renders into the DOM, passes every
			bUnit assertion, and is painted nowhere at all. It shipped that way once. Nothing else
			on this page collides; this one word does.
		*@
		@if (_toast is { } toast)
		{
			<div class="mode-toast @(_placingMarker ? "raised" : null)" role="status" aria-live="polite">@toast</div>
		}

		@*
			Who is just up the road and just behind (§5.4), down the free edge of the map.

			The one thing on this screen that is read rather than acted on and still earns space
			over the map, and the reason is what "the map is the page" is for: a rider looking at
			their own pin among four others cannot tell a group that is together from one that is
			strung out over two kilometres, because the map has no scale they are reading at speed.
			This is that answer in numbers, and it is the question the whole ride turns on.

			Everything else that reads rather than acts is one tap away under "Info" and the members
			screen, which is where a rider goes at a stop. This is for the ones taken while moving.

			Off by a menu item rather than permanent: it covers a strip of map, and a rider on a
			ride with no route or on their own has nothing to read in it. Empty renders nothing at
			all — see NeighbourPanel.
		*@
		@if (_showNeighbours)
		{
			<NeighbourPanel Rows="_neighbours" />
		}

		@* Catches the tap that dismisses the menu. It covers the map and nothing else — over
		   the nav rail too and a tap meant for Home would only close a menu. *@
		@if (_menuOpen)
		{
			<div class="menu-backdrop" @onclick="CloseMenu"></div>
		}

		<div class="chrome">
			@*
				The way in to everything this screen does that is not a camera mode.

				It carried a dot once, for whichever mode was still set from inside the menu. Nothing
				is any more — both modes are lit buttons directly below it — and a second indicator
				for a state a button beside it already shows is the one that goes stale. Removing it
				also takes the last reason for this button to have two labels.
			*@
			<button type="button" class="hamburger @(_menuOpen ? "open" : null)"
					@onclick="ToggleMenu"
					aria-haspopup="menu"
					aria-expanded="@(_menuOpen ? "true" : "false")"
					aria-label="Adventure actions">
				<i class="fa fa-bars" aria-hidden="true"></i>
			</button>

			@*
				"Centre on me and keep me there", on the map rather than in the menu (§5.3).

				One of the two things a rider does to this map while moving — heading-up, directly
				below, is the other — and everything the menu holds is done at a stop, so these are
				the two that have earned a permanent 48 px target instead of a tap, a read and a
				second tap. It is also the *only* way to set following: the duplicate under the rule
				in the menu was removed once this existed, because a mode with two controls is a
				mode a rider has to check twice.

				aria-pressed rather than a role, because this is a toggle button and not a menu
				item — and it is what a screen reader reads out as the state, which matters more
				here than anywhere else on the page since the visual state is a colour swap.

				The label carries the "waiting for a fix" case the menu item used to state in
				words. Following with nothing to point at is not a fault — a ride that has not
				started carries no positions (§5.1) — but a lit button over a map that is not
				moving needs to be able to say which of the two it is.
			*@
			<button type="button" class="follow @(_followMe ? "on" : null)"
					@onclick="ToggleFollowMeAsync"
					aria-pressed="@(_followMe ? "true" : "false")"
					aria-label="@FollowLabel">
				<i class="fa fa-location-crosshairs" aria-hidden="true"></i>
			</button>

			@*
				"Which way up is the map" — north, or the way this rider is pointing — on the map
				rather than in the menu, for the same reason following is.

				It sat under the rule in the menu until now, on the argument that it is set once at
				the top of a ride and then left alone. That was the wrong half of the pairing to
				notice: it is the *other* mode that turns the map under a rider after the tap that
				set it, so a rider who wants it off — because the map is swinging at a roundabout,
				or because they are trying to read a street name — is asking at exactly the moment
				a tap, a read and a second tap costs the most. Both camera modes now sit in the same
				stack, which is also what lets a rider see the two states at once instead of opening
				a menu to check one of them.

				aria-pressed and not menuitemcheckbox, because it is a toggle button now and no
				longer an item in a menu — and the state it reads out is what a screen reader gets
				in place of the colour swap.

				Two glyphs rather than one that changes colour: colour alone is the first thing to
				go through a visor in daylight (§18.6), and this button is read at a glance while
				moving. The label carries the "waiting for a heading" case the menu item used to
				state in words — a lit button over a map that is not turning needs to say which of
				the two it is, exactly as the follow button does.

				The base map keeps its own compass on the opposite edge, and the two do not
				disagree: the compass says which way the map is pointing and turns it back to north,
				this says whether the map is being turned *for the rider*. Tapping the compass is a
				gesture like any other, and takes this mode off with it (OnMapGestureAsync).
			*@
			<button type="button" class="heading-up @(_headingUp ? "on" : null)"
					@onclick="ToggleHeadingUpAsync"
					aria-pressed="@(_headingUp ? "true" : "false")"
					aria-label="@HeadingUpLabel">
				<i class="fa @(_headingUp ? "fa-compass" : "fa-location-arrow")" aria-hidden="true"></i>
			</button>

			@if (_menuOpen)
			{
				<div class="menu" role="menu">
					<button type="button" role="menuitem" @onclick="GoToInfo">
						<i class="fa fa-fw fa-circle-info" aria-hidden="true"></i> Info
					</button>

					@if (ride.Permissions.AllowMemberMarkers || ride.IsOrganiser)
					{
						@* Arms the map rather than opening the composer: the point is chosen here,
						   on the map the rider is already reading. *@
						<button type="button" role="menuitem" @onclick="StartPlacingMarker">
							<i class="fa fa-fw fa-circle-plus" aria-hidden="true"></i> Add marker
						</button>
					}

					@*
						No "quiet while live" note beside this any more. It survived v0.26's removal
						of the Live silence and was then simply wrong — the thread notifies in every
						ride state (§17.6), so the label was telling riders the opposite of what the
						app does.
					*@
					<button type="button" role="menuitem" @onclick="GoToThread">
						<i class="fa fa-fw fa-comments" aria-hidden="true"></i> Adventure thread
					</button>

					<hr />

					@*
						Below the rule: what the map itself is doing, as opposed to where the
						menu can take you. A checkbox rather than a plain item, because it is a
						mode that stays on after the menu closes — role="menuitemcheckbox" is
						what tells a screen reader that, and aria-checked is what carries it.

						The panel down the left edge naming who is just ahead and just behind (§5.4).

						The only mode left in here, now that both camera modes are buttons in the
						stack outside. It belongs here and they do not, for a reason that is not
						about how often it is set: it does not move the map. A rider who has
						forgotten this one is on is looking at a panel that says so; a rider who has
						forgotten one of those is looking at a map moving by itself, which is why
						they are on screen permanently and this is a line in a menu.

						It says out loud when it can have nothing to show. "Along the route" needs a
						route, and a rider who turns this on for a ride that has none would otherwise
						be looking at an empty edge of map wondering which of the two failed.
					*@
					<button type="button" role="menuitemcheckbox" class="neighbours @(_showNeighbours ? "checked" : null)"
							aria-checked="@(_showNeighbours ? "true" : "false")"
							@onclick="ToggleNeighboursAsync">
						<i class="fa fa-fw fa-people-group" aria-hidden="true"></i>
						Nearby travellers
						@if (_showNeighbours && _session.RoutePolyline is null)
						{
							<span class="quiet">needs a route</span>
						}
					</button>

					<button type="button" role="menuitem" @onclick="ShowMarkers">
						<i class="fa fa-fw fa-map-pin" aria-hidden="true"></i> Markers
						<span class="count">@_session.Markers.Count</span>
					</button>

					@*
						What this device's own receiver is doing (§4.3). Not a control — sharing is
						a consent decision and lives on the info page beside the words describing
						it — but a rider mid-ride needs to be able to answer "am I on their map?"
						without leaving the map, and the honest answer is not "the switch is on".
					*@
					@*
						Absent on a host with no receiver (§18.6) rather than reporting that it has
						none: a rider on a laptop is watching other people's pins, and a line in
						their own menu saying "this device cannot share its location" answers a
						question they were never asking.
					*@
					@if (_broadcast is { } receiver)
					{
						<div class="gps" role="status">
							<i class="fa fa-fw @BroadcastIcon" aria-hidden="true"></i>
							<span>@receiver.Describe()</span>
						</div>
					}
				</div>
			}
		</div>

		@*
			The map's pins carry an icon and a title and nothing else — the Skia overlay is
			`pointer-events: none` so gestures reach the base map (RideMap), which means a pin
			cannot be tapped for its detail. This list is where a marker's note, its photo and
			its delete live (§16.4, §16.5). It used to be a permanent sidebar; on a full-screen
			map it is what the menu's "Markers" opens.
		*@
		@if (_showMarkers)
		{
			<div class="overlay markers-dialog" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-label="Markers"
				 @onclick="CloseMarkers">
				<div class="overlay-card markers" @onclick:stopPropagation="true">
					<header>
						<h2>Markers</h2>
						<button type="button" class="close" @onclick="CloseMarkers" aria-label="Close">×</button>
					</header>

					@if (_session.Markers.Count == 0)
					{
						<p class="hint">Nothing marked on this adventure yet.</p>
					}
					else
					{
						<ul class="marker-list">
							@foreach (MarkerDto marker in _session.OrderedMarkers)
							{
								bool open = _openMarkerId == marker.Id;

								<li class="@(open ? "open" : null)">
									<button type="button" class="marker-head"
											aria-expanded="@(open ? "true" : "false")"
											@onclick="() => ToggleMarkerAsync(marker)">
										<img class="glyph" alt="" src="@MarkerIconGlyphs.AssetPath(marker.Icon)" />
										<span class="what">
											@* A title is optional (§16.2). On the map that means a bare icon, which is
											   the point of it; in a list it would mean a row with nothing in the name
											   column, so the icon's own name stands in. *@
											<strong>@MarkerIconGlyphs.Name(marker.Title, marker.Icon)</strong>
											<em>@marker.CreatedByUserName</em>
										</span>
										<span class="badges">
											@if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(marker.Note))
											{
												<span title="Has a note">📝</span>
											}
											@if (marker.PhotoId is not null)
											{
												<span title="Has a photo">📷</span>
											}
										</span>
									</button>

									@if (open)
									{
										<MarkerDetails Marker="marker" CanDelete="_session.CanDelete(marker)"
													   Deleting="_deletingMarkerId == marker.Id"
													   OnDelete="DeleteMarkerAsync" />
									}
								</li>
							}
						</ul>
					}
				</div>
			</div>
		}

		@if (_showConsent)
		{
			<ConsentPrompt RideName="@ride.Name"
						   OnShare="OnConsentShareAsync"
						   OnDismiss="OnConsentDismissAsync" />
		}

		@if (NearbyMarkers is { Count: > 0 } nearby)
		{
			<div class="overlay nearby-dialog" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-label="Markers near that point"
				 @onclick="CloseNearby">
				<div class="overlay-card" @onclick:stopPropagation="true">
					<header>
						<h2>
							@(nearby.Count == 1 ? "1 marker here" : $"{nearby.Count} markers here")
						</h2>
						<button type="button" class="close" @onclick="CloseNearby" aria-label="Close">×</button>
					</header>

					@foreach (MarkerDto marker in nearby)
					{
						<section class="nearby-marker">
							<h3>
								<img class="glyph" alt="" src="@MarkerIconGlyphs.AssetPath(marker.Icon)" />
								@MarkerIconGlyphs.Name(marker.Title, marker.Icon)
							</h3>
							<p class="by">@marker.CreatedByUserName</p>

							<MarkerDetails Marker="marker" CanDelete="_session.CanDelete(marker)"
										   Deleting="_deletingMarkerId == marker.Id"
										   OnDelete="DeleteMarkerAsync" />
						</section>
					}
				</div>
			</div>
		}
	}
</div>

@code {
	[Parameter] public Guid RideId { get; set; }

	/// <summary>
	/// Where a ride opens when this device has nothing stored for it. Sydney, at city scale —
	/// the same view this page has always opened on, kept as a constant now that
	/// <see cref="RestoreViewAsync"/> and a change of ride both need something to go back to.
	/// </summary>
	private static readonly MapCamera DefaultCamera = new(-33.868, 151.209, 11);

	/// <summary>
	/// How often a pan may reach the device store. The map reports a viewport on every frame of
	/// a drag, and <c>localStorage</c> behind JS interop is not a per-frame surface — so the view
	/// is held in memory and written at this cadence, plus once on the way out
	/// (<see cref="DisposeAsync"/>), which is the write that actually matters.
	/// </summary>
	private static readonly TimeSpan SaveInterval = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);

	/// <summary>
	/// How long a mode change stays on screen. Long enough to be read at a glance by somebody who
	/// is also riding a bike, short enough that it is gone before it becomes furniture.
	/// </summary>
	private static readonly TimeSpan ToastDuration = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(3.5);

	/// <summary>
	/// How fast this rider has to be going before their heading is allowed to turn the map, in
	/// metres per second — about 5 km/h, a walk.
	/// <para>
	/// A GPS heading is derived from successive fixes, so below walking pace it is not a direction
	/// at all: it is the noise between two readings of a bike leaning against a fence, and it
	/// arrives once a second. Honouring it would spin the map under a rider stopped at a light,
	/// which is both useless and the exact moment they are most likely to be reading it. Under the
	/// threshold the map holds the bearing it had.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private const double HeadingSpeedFloorMps = 1.5;

	/// <summary>
	/// How far the rider's heading has to swing before the map is turned to match, in degrees.
	/// <para>
	/// The rotation counterpart of <see cref="_followedTo"/>'s exact comparison, and a threshold
	/// rather than equality because a heading is never twice the same number. Small enough that a
	/// corner is followed as it is taken; large enough that a straight road does not cost a JS
	/// interop call and a full overlay repaint every second.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private const double HeadingChangeDeg = 3;

	private RideSession _session = default!;
	private MapCamera _camera = DefaultCamera;
	private MapViewport? _mapViewport;
	private readonly Dictionary<Guid, MapMarker> _markerLayer = new();

	/// <summary>
	/// Whether the map keeps this rider centred as their fixes arrive (§5.3).
	/// <para>
	/// <strong>A pan cancels it</strong>, and says so (see <see cref="OnMapGestureAsync"/>). That
	/// used to be impossible and the reason was written here: telling a rider's drag apart from the
	/// mode's own move cannot be done from the viewport event, which reports where the map is and
	/// never who put it there — so the mode stayed on and the map undid the rider's pan a second
	/// later. <see cref="MapGesture"/> is that missing half, straight from MapLibre's own
	/// <c>originalEvent</c>, and with it "the rider took hold of the map" is a fact rather than a
	/// guess. What made a self-cancelling mode unacceptable was that it was silent; the toast is
	/// what pays for it.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private bool _followMe;

	/// <summary>
	/// Whether the map is turned to this rider's heading rather than held north-up.
	/// <para>
	/// A separate switch from <see cref="_followMe"/> — one decides where the screen is centred,
	/// the other which way the ground is turned — but <strong>the controls never leave this one on
	/// without that one</strong>. The map rotates about the centre of the screen, so a turning map
	/// the rider is not at the centre of swings the ground around a point that is nobody, which is
	/// worse than north-up rather than better.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// That single fact is enforced from both ends and there are only two:
	/// <see cref="ToggleHeadingUpAsync"/> switches following on with this, and every route that
	/// switches following off goes through <see cref="StopFollowing"/>, which takes this with it.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// A stored view can still name the pair — an older build wrote them independently — and
	/// <see cref="RestoreViewAsync"/> reads back what it was given rather than correcting it. That
	/// is deliberate: the alternative is a map that opens somewhere the device never said, and the
	/// first thing the rider does to such a map puts the pairing back anyway.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// The reverse is not true and must not be: following on its own is an ordinary north-up map
	/// that keeps the rider on screen, which is what most riders want. So a twist of the map, or a
	/// tap on the base map's compass, cancels this and leaves following alone — both are somebody
	/// stating which way up they want the map, and neither says anything about the centre.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// It holds its bearing at a standstill rather than following a stationary receiver's heading;
	/// see <see cref="MyHeadingDeg"/> for why a parked bike must not be allowed to spin the map.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private bool _headingUp;

	/// <summary>
	/// Where the camera was last sent to follow this rider. Compared against the newest fix so a
	/// stationary rider costs nothing: positions arrive about once a second whether or not anybody
	/// has moved, and each one would otherwise be a JS interop call to re-centre on the same point.
	/// </summary>
	private (double Latitude, double Longitude)? _followedTo;

	/// <summary>
	/// The bearing the camera was last turned to for <see cref="_headingUp"/>. The rotation half of
	/// <see cref="_followedTo"/>, and needed for the same reason — but against a threshold rather
	/// than for equality, because a heading is a noisy number and an exact comparison would let
	/// every fix through.
	/// </summary>
	private double? _aimedBearing;

	/// <summary>
	/// What the map is telling the rider it has just started or stopped doing, or null when there
	/// is nothing to say. Cleared by <see cref="_toastTimer"/>.
	/// </summary>
	private string? _toast;

	/// <summary>
	/// Takes the toast back off the screen. One timer, restarted per message: a second toast
	/// replaces the first rather than queueing behind it, because the newer one is by definition
	/// the truer description of what the map is doing now.
	/// </summary>
	private ITimer? _toastTimer;

	/// <summary>How many toasts this page has shown. Only ever compared for equality — see <see cref="ShowToast"/>.</summary>
	private int _toastSequence;

	/// <summary>The view last written to the device store, so an unchanged one is not written twice.</summary>
	private LiveMapView? _savedView;

	/// <summary>The view waiting to be written, if the throttle has not let one through yet.</summary>
	private LiveMapView? _pendingView;

	private DateTimeOffset _lastSavedUtc;

	/// <summary>
	/// The ride's planned routes as the overlay draws them (§5.4), in the session's order so a
	/// route's colour matches the one the info page's list gives it.
	/// </summary>
	private IReadOnlyList<RouteOverlay> _routeLayer = [];

	/// <summary>
	/// This rider's own private area (§10.1), drawn so they can see where the map stops carrying
	/// them. Null when none is set, which is every device by default.
	/// <para>
	/// <strong>Nobody else sees this.</strong> It is drawn from the device's own store and is not
	/// part of the ride's data — the server has never been told it exists, so there is nothing for
	/// another member's client to draw even if it wanted to.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private IReadOnlyList<MapCircle>? _privateAreaLayer;

	private bool _menuOpen;
	private bool _showMarkers;
	private bool _showConsent;

	/// <summary>
	/// Whether the panel naming the riders immediately ahead and behind is on screen (§5.4).
	/// <para>
	/// <strong>On unless the rider has turned it off</strong>, which is the opposite default from the
	/// two camera modes and deliberately so: those move the map under somebody who did not ask, and
	/// this covers a corner of it and takes no tap. A read-out nobody knows exists is one nobody
	/// turns on. See <see cref="LiveMapView.Neighbours"/>, which is where the default lives for a
	/// device that has never expressed a preference.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// Persisted with the view and carried across rides, like following and heading-up: whether a
	/// rider wants the group's shape on screen is a standing preference, not a fact about one ride.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private bool _showNeighbours = true;

	/// <summary>
	/// The rows that panel draws, rebuilt when something moves rather than per render.
	/// <para>
	/// Deriving them costs one projection onto the route per member (§5.4), and this page renders
	/// several times a second on a phone that is sharing — most of those for reasons that cannot have
	/// changed anybody's place on the road. So it is rebuilt exactly where the map layer is, from the
	/// two events that can move a rider: a position batch, and this device's own fix.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private IReadOnlyList<NeighbourRow> _neighbours = [];

	/// <summary>
	/// Whether this page is currently one of the screen lock's holders (§4.3), so it releases
	/// exactly what it took and no more. See <see cref="HoldScreenAsync"/>.
	/// </summary>
	private bool _holdingScreen;

	/// <summary>
	/// Whether this ride has already been dropped from the device's memory of "the ride you are
	/// on" (§18.6). One ride's disappearance is one decision, and the session raises for plenty
	/// of other reasons after it.
	/// </summary>
	private bool _forgotten;

	/// <summary>
	/// Whether the next tap on the map is a marker's point rather than a question about what is
	/// already there (§16.1).
	/// <para>
	/// "Add marker" arms this instead of opening the composer, because the point is a thing the
	/// rider picks on the map they are already reading. The composer used to open on the centre
	/// of that view, which is a guess — and the one place a rider never wants to mark is the
	/// middle of their own screen, because that is where they are.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// It survives nothing: a change of ride clears it, and so does the tap that spends it. The
	/// strip over the map is the only thing that says it is on, so it must never outlive that.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private bool _placingMarker;

	/// <summary>Which marker's detail is expanded, or null when the list is all collapsed.</summary>
	private Guid? _openMarkerId;

	/// <summary>
	/// The markers a tap on the map landed on, nearest first — identified by id rather than
	/// held as DTOs so the popup follows the session's marker set. An edit arriving on the hub
	/// updates what is on screen, and a marker deleted underneath simply drops out; keeping
	/// copies would leave the popup showing a marker nobody else can see any more.
	/// </summary>
	private IReadOnlyList<Guid> _nearbyMarkerIds = Array.Empty<Guid>();

	private RideMap? _map;

	/// <summary>
	/// The marker whose delete is in flight. Deliberately not the session's <c>Busy</c>: that
	/// one disables the sharing switch and the organiser's lifecycle buttons over on the info
	/// page, and none of those have anything to do with removing a pin.
	/// </summary>
	private Guid? _deletingMarkerId;

	private string Title => _session?.Ride?.Name ?? "Adventure";

	/// <summary>
	/// How old the copy on screen is, in the words the offline strip uses (§4.4).
	/// <para>
	/// Coarse on purpose, and coarser the older it gets: "18 min ago" and "19 min ago" are the
	/// same fact to somebody deciding whether to trust a pin, and a number that ticks over every
	/// second is a thing to watch rather than a thing to read. The units are what carry the
	/// meaning — minutes is a group that has probably not gone far, hours is not.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// Recomputed on render rather than on a timer of its own. On a phone that is sharing, the
	/// receiver re-renders this page about once a second and the label keeps up; on one that is
	/// not, it can sit still while the copy ages — which is a stale reading of a stale copy, and
	/// the reason the sentence never says "just now" for anything but the first minute.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private string CachedAge
	{
		get
		{
			if (_session.CachedUtc is not { } cached)
			{
				return "when you last had signal";
			}

			// Negative when the device's clock has moved backwards under a stored stamp — a manual
			// change, or a phone that picked up network time after being off for a week. Clamped
			// rather than rendered, because "in 3 hours" is not a thing a cache can be.
			TimeSpan age = Clock.GetUtcNow() - cached;

			return age switch
			{
				{ TotalMinutes: < 1 } => "a moment ago",
				{ TotalHours: < 1 } => $"{(int)age.TotalMinutes} min ago",
				{ TotalDays: < 1 } => $"{(int)age.TotalHours} h ago",
				_ => $"{(int)age.TotalDays} d ago",
			};
		}
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// What the follow button says it is for, and — once it is on — what it is currently managing
	/// to do.
	/// <para>
	/// The third case is the one that has to be there. Following with nothing to point at is not a
	/// fault: a ride that has not started carries no positions (§5.1), and a receiver that has not
	/// got a lock yet has nothing of its own either. It used to be stated in words beside the menu
	/// item, and now that the button is the only control it is the button that has to say it — a
	/// lit control over a map that is not moving otherwise reads as broken.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private string FollowLabel => (_followMe, MyPoint) switch
	{
		(false, _) => "Centre on me and follow",
		(true, null) => "Stop following me — waiting for a fix",
		_ => "Stop following me",
	};

	/// <summary>
	/// What the heading-up button says it is for, and — once it is on — whether it has anything to
	/// turn the map by.
	/// <para>
	/// The third case is the counterpart of <see cref="FollowLabel"/>'s and is there for the same
	/// reason. A map that is lit for heading-up and sitting square to north is not broken: it is a
	/// rider stopped at a light, or one whose receiver has not produced a heading yet, and
	/// <see cref="MyHeadingDeg"/> deliberately answers null for both so the map holds its bearing.
	/// The menu item used to state that in words beside itself; now that the control is a glyph,
	/// the label is the only place left that can.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private string HeadingUpLabel => (_headingUp, MyHeadingDeg) switch
	{
		(false, _) => "Turn the map to my heading",
		(true, null) => "North up — waiting for a heading",
		_ => "North up",
	};

	/// <summary>
	/// The glyph beside the receiver's state in the menu. Three shapes rather than one that
	/// changes colour: the map is read through a visor in daylight, and colour alone is the first
	/// thing to go (§18.6).
	/// </summary>
	private string BroadcastIcon => _broadcast?.Status switch
	{
		LocationBroadcastStatus.Broadcasting => "fa-satellite-dish",
		LocationBroadcastStatus.Suppressed => "fa-house-lock",
		LocationBroadcastStatus.Starting or LocationBroadcastStatus.WaitingForFix => "fa-spinner",
		LocationBroadcastStatus.Off => "fa-location-crosshairs",
		_ => "fa-triangle-exclamation",
	};

	/// <summary>
	/// The markers the last tap landed on, resolved against the live set. Null when nothing
	/// was tapped, so the popup is simply absent rather than an empty card.
	/// </summary>
	private IReadOnlyList<MarkerDto>? NearbyMarkers
	{
		get
		{
			if (_nearbyMarkerIds.Count == 0)
			{
				return null;
			}

			List<MarkerDto> found = new(_nearbyMarkerIds.Count);
			foreach (Guid id in _nearbyMarkerIds)
			{
				if (_session.Markers.TryGetValue(id, out MarkerDto? marker))
				{
					found.Add(marker);
				}
			}

			return found.Count == 0 ? null : found;
		}
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// This rider's own latest fix, or null when the ride has none for them.
	/// <para>
	/// The ride's own view of where they are — the same fix everyone else's map is drawing them
	/// at — rather than a second read of the device's GPS. Following the pin the rider can see is
	/// the behaviour the mode promises, and it works on every host: <see cref="ILocationProvider"/>
	/// does not exist in a browser (§18.6), and a map that followed a private read would put the
	/// rider somewhere their own suppressed position (§10.1) says nobody should be looking.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// Null while not sharing, and until the first fix lands. That is what the follow button's
	/// "waiting for a fix" label says, rather than the mode looking broken.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private RiderPositionDto? MyPosition =>
		Auth.UserId is { } me && _session.Positions.TryGetValue(me, out RiderPositionDto? mine) ? mine : null;

	/// <summary>
	/// Whether this host reads its own GPS — and therefore whether the ride's copy of this rider is
	/// allowed onto this map at all.
	/// <para>
	/// <strong>The one thing that decides it, in both places it is decided.</strong> On a phone the
	/// device's own fix is available and is strictly better, so the round-tripped copy is refused
	/// outright. In a browser there is no receiver (§18.6) and the server's copy is the only answer
	/// there has ever been, so refusing it would take a rider who is sharing from their phone off
	/// the map they are watching on a laptop, for nothing.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private bool HasOwnReceiver => _broadcast?.IsSupported == true;

	/// <summary>
	/// Where this rider is, for this rider's own map: <strong>this device's own reading on a host
	/// that has one, and the ride's copy only on a host that does not</strong> (see
	/// <see cref="HasOwnReceiver"/>).
	/// <para>
	/// <strong>Why a phone refuses the round trip rather than merely preferring the local read.</strong>
	/// The ride's copy is the same fix after a journey: filtered by <c>PositionGate</c> on the way
	/// out (§4.2), kept only once the ride is <c>Live</c> (§5.1), and returned on a 5 s fan-out
	/// tick (§5.3). Every one of those is right for showing the <em>other</em> riders — it is the
	/// best anyone can know about somebody else — and every one is a reason not to show it back to
	/// the person whose phone produced it, who has the current answer in their hand. Preferring the
	/// device and falling back to the server was worse than either: the mark would track properly,
	/// then jump backwards to a stale point the moment the GPS blinked.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// The privacy rule that first put the ride's copy here survives on the host it applies to,
	/// because it is enforced a layer down: <c>LocationBroadcastState.OwnFix</c> answers null while
	/// the fix is suppressed by the private area (§10.1), so a phone following this never goes
	/// anywhere the rider's own setting says nobody should be looking. In a browser the question
	/// does not arise — a fix that was suppressed was never sent, so the server has nothing to
	/// return.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private (double Latitude, double Longitude)? MyPoint =>
		HasOwnReceiver
			? _broadcast?.OwnFix is { } own ? (own.Latitude, own.Longitude) : null
			: MyPosition is { } mine ? (PositionScale.ToDegrees(mine.Lat), PositionScale.ToDegrees(mine.Lon)) : null;

	/// <summary>
	/// Which way this rider is travelling, for a map that is turned to match — or null when
	/// nothing on screen should be turned by it.
	/// <para>
	/// Split between the device's own reading and the ride's copy on exactly the same rule as
	/// <see cref="MyPoint"/>, and for the same reasons: a phone has the current answer in its hand,
	/// and a browser has only ever had the server's.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// <strong>Null is the important case and it has two causes, both of which mean "hold".</strong>
	/// A fix with no heading at all is the receiver saying it does not know, which is never zero —
	/// zero is due north (§8). And a heading under <see cref="HeadingSpeedFloorMps"/> is arithmetic
	/// on a bike that is not moving. Answering null for both leaves <see cref="AimCameraAsync"/>
	/// with nothing to turn to, so the map keeps the bearing it had, which is what a rider stopped
	/// at a light wants and is not what "the last direction we are confident about" would have to
	/// be stored separately to achieve.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private double? MyHeadingDeg
	{
		get
		{
			double? speed = HasOwnReceiver ? _broadcast?.OwnFix?.SpeedMps : MyPosition?.SpeedMps;
			double? heading = HasOwnReceiver ? _broadcast?.OwnFix?.HeadingDeg : MyPosition?.HeadingDeg;

			// Null-propagating: a fix with no speed reads as not moving, exactly as the overlay's
			// choice of a stopped dot over a heading arrow does (§16.3).
			return speed >= HeadingSpeedFloorMps ? heading : null;
		}
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// The line §5.4 projects riders against, the way round this device reads it.
	/// <para>
	/// The same derivation the members screen and the info page's gap list use, and it has to be:
	/// reversing a route changes which end "distance along" is measured from, so a panel ranked off
	/// the stored order while the overlay draws its chevrons the other way would put the rider off
	/// the front at the bottom of the list.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private IReadOnlyList<TrackPoint>? GapRoute
	{
		get
		{
			if (_session.RoutePolyline is not { } line)
			{
				return null;
			}

			// Routes[0] is the route RoutePolyline was derived from — see RideSession.ApplyRoutes.
			return _session.Routes.Count > 0 && RouteStyles.IsReversed(_session.Routes[0].TrackId)
				? [.. line.Reverse()]
				: line;
		}
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Where this rider is along the planned route, or null when that cannot be answered — no route,
	/// or no fix of this device's own yet.
	/// <para>
	/// Projected from <see cref="MyPoint"/> rather than read off the ride's copy of this rider, and
	/// the distinction is the whole value of the panel: on a phone the ride's copy is this fix after
	/// a round trip (§4.2, §5.3), so measuring everybody else from it would show the group drifting
	/// forward past a rider holding a steady wheel. Every gap in the panel is a difference against
	/// this number, so it is the one that has to be current.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private double? MyAlongMetres =>
		GapRoute is { } line && MyPoint is { } me
			? GapCalculator.Project(line, new TrackPoint(me.Latitude, me.Longitude))?.AlongMetres
			: null;

	/// <summary>
	/// This device drawn as itself (§4.3), or null when there is no fix of its own to draw — the
	/// web, a receiver that is off or still warming up, or a fix inside the private area.
	/// <para>
	/// Only ever built from the device's own read; see <see cref="MyPoint"/> for why the ride's
	/// copy of this rider is not an acceptable substitute.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private MapMarker? SelfMarker => _broadcast?.OwnFix is { } fix
		? new MapMarker(
			// Keyed on the rider rather than on a sentinel, so this occupies the same slot the
			// server's copy of them would have — belt and braces behind RebuildMapLayer's skip.
			Id: Auth.UserId ?? Guid.Empty,
			Latitude: fix.Latitude,
			Longitude: fix.Longitude,
			IconKey: string.Empty,
			Title: string.Empty,
			DirectionDeg: fix.HeadingDeg,
			Kind: MarkerKind.Self,
			SpeedMps: fix.SpeedMps)
		: null;

	/// <summary>Zoom a marker is brought to when it is chosen. Street level, not suburb level.</summary>
	private const double FocusZoom = 16;

	/// <summary>
	/// This device's receiver, or <c>null</c> on a host that has none — both browsers (§18.6).
	/// <para>
	/// Resolved rather than <c>@@inject</c>ed because the web hosts do not register it at all: GPS
	/// is a mobile concern, and a stub answering "not supported" was a registration kept alive so
	/// one <c>@@inject</c> could succeed. Null is a state this page already knew how to draw — see
	/// <see cref="HasOwnReceiver"/>, which is what decides between this device's own fix and the
	/// ride's round-tripped copy of it.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private LocationBroadcastState? _broadcast;

	/// <summary>
	/// The device's private area (§10.1), or <c>null</c> on a host with no receiver for it to
	/// gate. The circle is drawn on this map so a rider can see how far it reaches; a browser has
	/// nothing to draw, because nothing there was ever going to be suppressed by it.
	/// </summary>
	private PrivateAreaState? _privateAreas;

	protected override void OnInitialized()
	{
		_broadcast = Services.GetService<LocationBroadcastState>();
		_privateAreas = Services.GetService<PrivateAreaState>();

		// RideSession has taken a nullable broadcaster from the start — see its constructor.
		_session = new RideSession(Api, Hub, Auth, _broadcast, Cache);
		_session.Changed += OnSessionChanged;

		if (_broadcast is { } broadcast)
		{
			// The receiver's state is drawn on this screen — see the line under the menu and the
			// strip over the map — and it moves on its own timetable, not the session's.
			broadcast.Changed += OnBroadcastChanged;
		}

		if (_privateAreas is { } areas)
		{
			// The area can be placed, moved or removed on the settings screen while this page is
			// still mounted — PrivateAreaState is scoped, so it is the same instance — and the map
			// should agree with the setting without a reload.
			areas.Changed += OnPrivateAreaChanged;
			RebuildPrivateAreaLayer();
		}

		// A route reversed on the info page while this page is still mounted turns the neighbours
		// panel end for end (see GapRoute), and RouteStyleState is scoped, so it is the same
		// instance that page wrote to. This is also the event LoadAsync fires once the device's
		// stored style has been read, which is what fills the panel on a first render.
		RouteStyles.Changed += OnRouteStyleChanged;
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Reads what this device stored after first render: the private area, and the view this map
	/// was last left on. On the web the store is behind JS interop, which does not exist during
	/// the prerender pass. The area read is idempotent, so it costs nothing on a device the
	/// settings screen has already read.
	/// <para>
	/// Also where this page takes the screen (§4.3). Here rather than in <c>OnInitialized</c> for
	/// the same reason as the two reads above — this is the first point at which there is a device
	/// to talk to rather than a prerender — and it is paired with the release in
	/// <see cref="DisposeAsync"/>.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
	{
		if (!firstRender)
		{
			return;
		}

		if (_privateAreas is { } areas)
		{
			await areas.LoadAsync();
		}

		// Which way round this device reads the ride's route, which is what decides who the
		// neighbours panel calls "ahead". Idempotent, and the map overlay inside RideMap asks for
		// the same read — whichever of the two arrives first pays for it.
		await RouteStyles.LoadAsync();

		await RestoreViewAsync();
		await HoldScreenAsync();
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Asks the device to keep its screen on for as long as this map is the page (§4.3).
	/// <para>
	/// <strong>Unconditional, for the whole time the page is mounted.</strong> Not gated on the
	/// ride being <c>Live</c>, not on sharing, and not on the map having moved: a rider parked at
	/// the meeting point reading who has arrived is looking at the screen exactly as much as one
	/// doing eighty, and a lock that switches itself off at a state change is one that goes dark
	/// at the moment the ride starts. On a host without a screen to hold this is a no-op
	/// (§18.6) — see <see cref="IScreenWakeLock"/>.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// The flag guards the pairing rather than the lock: <c>OnAfterRenderAsync</c> and
	/// <c>DisposeAsync</c> are not guaranteed to both run, and releasing something this page never
	/// took would count down somebody else's hold.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private async Task HoldScreenAsync()
	{
		if (_holdingScreen)
		{
			return;
		}

		_holdingScreen = true;
		await ScreenWakeLock.RequestAsync();
	}

	/// <summary>Hands the screen back to the rider's own display timeout, if this page took it.</summary>
	private async Task ReleaseScreenAsync()
	{
		if (!_holdingScreen)
		{
			return;
		}

		_holdingScreen = false;
		await ScreenWakeLock.ReleaseAsync();
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// The device's receiver moved. On the phone this — not the ride's position batch — is what
	/// usually moves the map: this rider's own mark is drawn from the device's fix (see
	/// <see cref="SelfMarker"/>), the follow camera aims at it (see <see cref="MyPoint"/>) and the
	/// map's bearing comes off the same fix (see <see cref="MyHeadingDeg"/>), so a fix arriving has
	/// to rebuild the layer and re-aim, exactly as a hub batch does.
	/// </summary>
	private void OnBroadcastChanged() => _ = InvokeAsync(async () =>
	{
		RebuildMapLayer();
		StateHasChanged();

		// Cheap when the rider has neither moved nor turned — see _followedTo and _aimedBearing.
		await AimCameraAsync();
	});

	private void OnPrivateAreaChanged()
	{
		RebuildPrivateAreaLayer();
		_ = InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// The device's route style moved — which, for this page, means a route may have been turned
	/// end for end. The overlay redraws itself off the same event; this is the half of it the
	/// neighbours panel depends on, since reversing a line swaps who is ahead of whom.
	/// </summary>
	private void OnRouteStyleChanged()
	{
		RebuildNeighbours();
		_ = InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Projects the device's area into the overlay's circle list. A fresh list is what tells the
	/// canvas to repaint, so this runs on change rather than per render — the live map already
	/// repaints about once a second on positions alone.
	/// </summary>
	private void RebuildPrivateAreaLayer() =>
		_privateAreaLayer = _privateAreas?.Area is { } area
			? [new MapCircle(area.Latitude, area.Longitude, area.RadiusM)]
			: null;

	protected override async Task OnParametersSetAsync()
	{
		if (_session.RideId == RideId)
		{
			// Same ride, re-supplied parameters. Reloading here would drop every marker and
			// every position for the length of a round trip, for nothing.
			return;
		}

		// A different ride in the same component instance — the router reuses this page when only
		// the route parameter changes. The ride being left keeps the ground it was left on (it is
		// still _session.RideId at this point, which is what RememberViewAsync stamps), and the
		// camera goes back to the default: the stored view names one ride, and applying it to
		// another would open a Melbourne ride over Sydney.
		if (_session.RideId != Guid.Empty)
		{
			await RememberViewAsync(now: true);
			_camera = DefaultCamera;
			_mapViewport = null;
			_followedTo = null;

			// Forgotten with the camera, not with the mode: _headingUp and _followMe carry across
			// rides, and these two are only the record of where the map was last aimed *for* them.
			// Keeping them would leave a new ride's map refusing to turn until the rider's heading
			// happened to cross the threshold measured from the last ride's.
			_aimedBearing = null;
		}

		_openMarkerId = null;
		_nearbyMarkerIds = Array.Empty<Guid>();
		_menuOpen = false;
		_showMarkers = false;
		// A different ride is a different question of whether it still exists.
		_forgotten = false;
		// Armed on one ride, spent on another is a marker on the wrong ride.
		_placingMarker = false;
		// "Following off — you moved the map" is about a map that is no longer on screen.
		_toast = null;
		// One ride's riders and one ride's gaps. The load below refills it; leaving the old ride's
		// names on the edge of the new ride's map for the length of a round trip would be worse than
		// an empty edge, because every one of them is a claim about where somebody is right now.
		_neighbours = [];

		await _session.LoadAsync(RideId);

		// This is the ride the rail's globe leads back to from now on, and after the next launch
		// (§18.6). Written here rather than on the join screen because this is the one page every
		// route into a ride ends on — a code redeemed, a row tapped in the list, a link followed,
		// or the globe itself.
		//
		// Only once the snapshot has landed: a ride id that 404s or that this rider is not a member
		// of is not somewhere to be sent back to.
		if (_session.Ride is not null)
		{
			await CurrentRide.SetAsync(RideId);
		}
		else if (_session.RideUnavailable)
		{
			// The server said this ride is not there for this rider — deleted, or they have been
			// removed from it (§5.2). Forgotten, so the globe stops leading to a page that can only
			// answer "no such ride". Only this ride: see CurrentRideState.ForgetAsync.
			//
			// A load that failed any other way keeps its place. A tunnel is not a reason to forget
			// which ride somebody is on, and the rider who most needs the globe is the one whose
			// phone has just lost signal in the middle of a ride.
			_forgotten = true;
			await CurrentRide.ForgetAsync(RideId);
		}

		// Consent prompt: shown once per joined ride that we do not yet share with. §5.6's
		// rule is dismissing = "not now", which is a no-op — closing the modal simply removes it.
		if (!_session.Sharing && _session.Ride?.State == RideStateDto.Open)
		{
			_showConsent = true;
		}
	}

	private void OnSessionChanged()
	{
		RebuildMapLayer();
		_ = InvokeAsync(async () =>
		{
			StateHasChanged();

			// A removal can land while the map is open, not only between loads (§5.2). Same answer
			// as the load that 404s, and once: the session goes on raising for everything else, and
			// forgetting a ride is not something to re-decide on every frame.
			if (_session.RideUnavailable && !_forgotten)
			{
				_forgotten = true;
				await CurrentRide.ForgetAsync(RideId);
			}

			// Every position batch is a chance that this rider moved, and this is the only place
			// that learns about one. Cheap when they did not — see _followedTo.
			await AimCameraAsync();
		});
	}

	// -- The menu -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

	private void ToggleMenu() => _menuOpen = !_menuOpen;

	private void CloseMenu() => _menuOpen = false;

	private void GoToInfo()
	{
		_menuOpen = false;
		Nav.NavigateTo($"/group-rides/{RideId}/info");
	}

	private void GoToThread()
	{
		_menuOpen = false;
		Nav.NavigateTo($"/group-rides/{RideId}/thread");
	}

	private void ShowMarkers()
	{
		_menuOpen = false;
		_showMarkers = true;
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Puts the "who is around me" panel on the map, or takes it off (§5.4).
	/// <para>
	/// <strong>No toast, unlike the two modes below the same rule.</strong> Those change what the map
	/// does <em>after</em> the tap that set them, which is why a rider who has forgotten one is on
	/// reads the map moving by itself as a fault — the sentence is what pays for that. This one is
	/// visible the instant the menu closes over it: it is either there or it is not, and announcing
	/// a panel that is already on screen is one more thing to read while riding.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// Persisted immediately rather than at the throttle's convenience, on the same footing as the
	/// other two toggles: it is a deliberate choice, not a by-product of a drag.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private async Task ToggleNeighboursAsync()
	{
		_menuOpen = false;
		_showNeighbours = !_showNeighbours;

		// Switching it on has to fill it now. The rebuild otherwise waits on the next position batch,
		// which on a ride nobody is moving in — a group at the meeting point — is a panel that stays
		// empty for as long as the rider is prepared to look at it.
		RebuildNeighbours();

		await RememberViewAsync(now: true);
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Turns "keep me centred" on or off, and acts on it at once — a rider who has just panned
	/// away and switched it on is asking to be brought back now, not at the next fix.
	/// <para>
	/// Reached from the button over the map, and from nowhere else. It briefly had a duplicate
	/// under the rule in the menu; that went as soon as the button existed, because two controls
	/// for one mode is a mode a rider has to check twice — and the one they would check is the
	/// one on screen.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// <strong>Switching it off takes heading-up with it</strong> (see <see cref="StopFollowing"/>).
	/// Switching it on does not bring heading-up along, and should not: a north-up map that keeps
	/// the rider on screen is the ordinary case and what most riders are asking for.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// Persisted with the view, so it survives the trip to the info page and the next launch.
	/// Written immediately rather than at the throttle's convenience: it is a deliberate choice,
	/// not a by-product of a drag.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private async Task ToggleFollowMeAsync()
	{
		_menuOpen = false;

		if (_followMe)
		{
			(_, bool wasHeadingUp) = StopFollowing();

			// The map is left at whatever bearing it is on. The rider pressed "stop following",
			// not "north up" — straightening the map would be a second thing they did not ask for,
			// and the base map's own compass is a permanent control for exactly that.
			ShowToast($"{NameStopped(wasFollowing: true, wasHeadingUp)}.");
		}
		else
		{
			_followMe = true;

			// Forgetting where we last followed to is what makes switching it back on recentre even
			// when the rider has not moved since.
			_followedTo = null;

			ShowToast("Following you — the map stays centred as you travel.");
		}

		await AimCameraAsync();
		await RememberViewAsync(now: true);
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Takes following off, and heading-up with it. <strong>The one place either of those is ever
	/// switched off by something other than the rider choosing the opposite mode</strong> — the
	/// follow button, a pan, and choosing a marker to look at all come through here, so the pairing
	/// cannot hold on two of the three routes and quietly not on the third.
	/// <para>
	/// <strong>Why heading-up cannot outlive following.</strong> The map rotates about the centre of
	/// the screen. A turning map the rider is not at the centre of swings the ground around a point
	/// that is nobody, which is worse than a north-up map rather than better — so the moment the
	/// rider stops being the centre, there is nothing left for the rotation to mean. The same fact
	/// makes <see cref="ToggleHeadingUpAsync"/> switch following <em>on</em>; this is it read
	/// backwards.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// It only stops the modes. Moving the camera is the caller's business and none of the three
	/// callers do: two of them are reacting to the rider having just put the map somewhere, and the
	/// third is putting it on a marker itself.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	/// <returns>
	/// What was running before, so the caller can name it. Read here rather than by each caller
	/// beforehand, because a caller that read one of the two and forgot the other would produce a
	/// sentence that is quietly wrong about what just happened to the map.
	/// </returns>
	private (bool WasFollowing, bool WasHeadingUp) StopFollowing()
	{
		(bool wasFollowing, bool wasHeadingUp) = (_followMe, _headingUp);

		_followMe = false;
		_followedTo = null;
		_headingUp = false;
		_aimedBearing = null;

		return (wasFollowing, wasHeadingUp);
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Names the modes that have just stopped, as the opening of the sentence saying why. One
	/// wording for all three routes out — the button, a pan, and choosing a marker — because they
	/// are the same event to a rider, differing only in what caused it.
	/// </summary>
	/// <param name="wasFollowing">Whether the map had been keeping this rider centred.</param>
	/// <param name="wasHeadingUp">Whether it had been turning to their heading.</param>
	private static string NameStopped(bool wasFollowing, bool wasHeadingUp) => (wasFollowing, wasHeadingUp) switch
	{
		(true, true) => "Following and heading up off",
		(true, false) => "Following off",
		// Reachable without following: a device can have stored the pair, and older builds set it.
		_ => "Heading up off",
	};

	/// <summary>
	/// Swaps between a north-up map and one turned to this rider's heading, and acts on it now
	/// rather than at the next fix.
	/// <para>
	/// Reached from the button over the map, and from nowhere else. It lived under the rule in the
	/// menu until following got a button of its own; it went out to the stack beside that one for
	/// the reason given in the markup — both modes keep moving the map after the tap that set them,
	/// so both are read and changed while riding, and neither is reachable from two places.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// <strong>Choosing heading-up switches following on with it.</strong> The map rotates about
	/// the centre of the screen, so heading-up only says what it means when the rider <em>is</em>
	/// that centre: turned to somebody's heading while they sit off in a corner, the ground swings
	/// around a point that is nobody, which is worse than north-up rather than better. Asking for a
	/// map that behaves like a bike computer and being handed half of one — and having to find a
	/// second control to get the rest — is not a reading of the request anybody holds.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// One direction only. Turning heading-up back <em>off</em> leaves following exactly as it is:
	/// a north-up map that keeps the rider on screen is an ordinary thing to want, and it is what
	/// most riders are already using. The pairing is enforced from the other end instead — every
	/// route that stops following takes heading-up with it (<see cref="StopFollowing"/>), which is
	/// the same fact read backwards.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// <strong>Turning it off puts the map back to north, and that is the whole of the setting.</strong>
	/// "North up" is not the absence of a mode, it is the other choice — so choosing it moves the
	/// camera exactly as choosing the other one does. The rider is left looking at the ground they
	/// were already looking at; only the bearing changes.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// Note what is <em>not</em> here: the gesture path (<see cref="OnMapGestureAsync"/>) turns the
	/// mode off without straightening the map. A rider who has just turned the map by hand — or
	/// tapped the compass, which has already turned it to north itself — has said where they want
	/// the bearing, and a camera move on top of that would be the app arguing with them.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private async Task ToggleHeadingUpAsync()
	{
		_menuOpen = false;
		_headingUp = !_headingUp;

		// Same purpose as _followedTo above: forgetting where the map was last aimed is what makes
		// switching it back on turn the map now, rather than at the next heading past the threshold.
		_aimedBearing = null;

		if (_headingUp)
		{
			// See the remarks: a turning map is only legible with the rider at the centre of it.
			// Unconditional rather than "if it was off", so the forgotten _followedTo below applies
			// either way and the recentre happens now — a rider who chooses this from off in a
			// corner of the map is asking to be brought back, not to wait for the next fix.
			_followMe = true;
			_followedTo = null;
		}

		// One sentence for what is now one action, rather than two toasts racing each other for
		// the same slot. It names both halves because both are true and the rider only asked for
		// one of them.
		ShowToast(_headingUp
			? "Heading up — the map turns as you travel, centred on you."
			: "North up.");

		if (_headingUp)
		{
			await AimCameraAsync();
		}
		else
		{
			await StraightenAsync();
		}

		await RememberViewAsync(now: true);
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Turns the map back to north, keeping the ground and the zoom the rider is looking at. What
	/// choosing "North up" does; see <see cref="ToggleHeadingUpAsync"/>.
	/// </summary>
	private Task StraightenAsync() =>
		ApplyCameraAsync(new MapCamera(
			_mapViewport?.CentreLatitude ?? _camera.Latitude,
			_mapViewport?.CentreLongitude ?? _camera.Longitude,
			_mapViewport?.ZoomLevel ?? _camera.ZoomLevel,
			HeadingDeg: 0));

	/// <summary>
	/// The rider took hold of the map. Whatever the map was doing on its own that the gesture has
	/// made nonsense of stops, and says so (§5.3).
	/// <para>
	/// <strong>The two gestures are not symmetric, and the asymmetry is the whole rule.</strong>
	/// A drag takes both modes off; a twist takes only the bearing.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// A twist is a statement about which way up the map is and nothing else — the rider is still
	/// happy to be carried along with the group, so following survives it. A drag is a statement
	/// about where the centre of the screen is, and heading-up cannot survive losing that: the map
	/// rotates about the centre, so a turning map that is no longer centred on the rider swings the
	/// ground around a point that is nobody. That is the same fact that makes
	/// <see cref="ToggleHeadingUpAsync"/> switch following <em>on</em>, read in the other direction
	/// — the mode is only legible with the rider at the middle, so it goes when the middle does.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// Neither path moves the camera. The rider has just said where they want it, and straightening
	/// the map on top of a gesture would be the app arguing with the hand that made it.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// The toast is not decoration here, it is what makes a self-cancelling mode acceptable at all:
	/// the modes are set and then left, and a rider who has forgotten one is on reads its silent
	/// disappearance as the app having lost the setting. Saying which gesture did it also teaches
	/// the gesture — the next rider who wants the map back does not have to go looking.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// Nothing is written to the device store on this path beyond the ordinary throttled view save,
	/// which <see cref="OnMapViewportReportedAsync"/> is already doing for the same gesture — the
	/// mode's new value rides along in it.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	/// <param name="gesture">What the rider did.</param>
	private async Task OnMapGestureAsync(MapGesture gesture)
	{
		bool changed = false;

		if (gesture == MapGesture.Pan && (_followMe || _headingUp))
		{
			(bool wasFollowing, bool wasHeadingUp) = StopFollowing();

			ShowToast($"{NameStopped(wasFollowing, wasHeadingUp)} — you moved the map.");
			changed = true;
		}

		if (gesture == MapGesture.Rotate && _headingUp)
		{
			_headingUp = false;
			_aimedBearing = null;
			changed = true;
			// Names the mode that stopped, not the one that did not start. "North up is off" was
			// the first wording and it says the opposite of what happened: the map is no longer
			// being turned for the rider, which if anything is north-up coming back.
			ShowToast("Heading up off — you turned the map yourself.");
		}

		if (!changed)
		{
			// A pan with nothing following, or a turn on a map that was already north-up. The
			// gesture is the rider using the map, and the map has nothing to say about it.
			return;
		}

		// Deliberate enough to be worth a write of its own, on the same footing as the toggles:
		// the viewport report that accompanies this gesture is throttled, and a rider who pans and
		// then closes the app inside the window would otherwise find the mode back on.
		await RememberViewAsync(now: true);
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Puts a sentence over the map for <see cref="ToastDuration"/>. See the markup for why the
	/// two automatic modes are the only things on this screen that get one.
	/// <para>
	/// The timer is restarted rather than queued: two mode changes in quick succession are one
	/// rider changing their mind, and the second message is the true one.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	/// <param name="message">What the map has just started or stopped doing.</param>
	private void ShowToast(string message)
	{
		_toast = message;

		// Counted rather than compared against the message text: a rider who turns following off,
		// on and off again inside the window sees the same words twice, and an equality check would
		// let the first timer take the second one's toast off the screen early.
		int shown = ++_toastSequence;

		_toastTimer?.Dispose();
		_toastTimer = Clock.CreateTimer(
			_ => _ = InvokeAsync(() =>
			{
				// A later toast owns the slot now, and its own timer will clear it.
				if (_toastSequence != shown)
				{
					return;
				}

				_toast = null;
				StateHasChanged();
			}),
			state: null,
			dueTime: ToastDuration,
			period: Timeout.InfiniteTimeSpan);
	}

	private void CloseMarkers()
	{
		_showMarkers = false;
		_openMarkerId = null;
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Arms the map: the next tap is the marker's point (§16.1). The composer opens from that
	/// tap, not from this menu item.
	/// <para>
	/// Closes everything else on the way in. A popup left open over the map would be sitting on
	/// the very gesture the rider has just been asked for.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private void StartPlacingMarker()
	{
		_menuOpen = false;
		_showMarkers = false;
		_nearbyMarkerIds = Array.Empty<Guid>();
		_placingMarker = true;
	}

	private void CancelPlacingMarker() => _placingMarker = false;

	/// <summary>
	/// Opens the marker composer on the point the rider tapped (§16.1), so the composer starts
	/// with the pin already where they put it rather than on a guess at what they meant.
	/// <para>
	/// Rounded to the wire's five decimal places (<see cref="PositionScale"/>), so the number
	/// that travels is the one that will be stored.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// The zoom used to ride along as well, so the composer's own picker could re-open at the
	/// scale the point was chosen at. That picker is gone — this tap is now the only place the
	/// point is chosen — so nothing reads a zoom on that route and it is no longer sent.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// Built at tap time, not baked into an <c>href</c>: keeping one current would mean
	/// re-rendering this page on every pan.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private void OpenComposerAt(MapClick point)
	{
		_placingMarker = false;

		// GetUriWithQueryParameters handles invariant formatting and escaping, and survives a
		// route rename better than string concatenation.
		Nav.NavigateTo(Nav.GetUriWithQueryParameters($"/group-rides/{RideId}/markers/new", new Dictionary<string, object?>
		{
			["lat"] = Math.Round(point.Latitude, 5),
			["lon"] = Math.Round(point.Longitude, 5),
		}));
	}

	// -- The map ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

	/// <summary>
	/// Nothing on screen depends on the viewport until <see cref="GoToComposer"/> reads it — so no
	/// render here. What it does do is remember where the rider is looking, throttled, so the map
	/// re-opens on this ground rather than on the app's default.
	/// <para>
	/// Also the moment the base map first reports a frame, which is when a map opened with one of
	/// the automatic modes already on can finally act on it.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private async Task OnMapViewportReportedAsync(MapViewport viewport)
	{
		_mapViewport = viewport;

		// Only on the *first* frame each mode has seen — the null is what says "opened with this
		// on and never acted on it yet". Re-aiming on every report would be a camera move per
		// frame of the rider's own drag, which is the map fighting the hand that is moving it.
		if ((_followMe && _followedTo is null) || (_headingUp && _aimedBearing is null))
		{
			await AimCameraAsync();
		}

		await RememberViewAsync();
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Puts the camera where the two automatic modes say it should be: centred on this rider if
	/// <see cref="_followMe"/> is on, turned to their heading if <see cref="_headingUp"/> is, and
	/// left exactly as the rider had it in whichever of the two axes is nobody's business.
	/// <para>
	/// <strong>One method for both, because they share a camera.</strong> Two would each have to
	/// send a whole <see cref="MapCamera"/> — there is no "set the bearing only" — so each would
	/// overwrite the other's axis with a stale copy, and a rider being followed round a corner
	/// would watch the map snap back and forth between two half-right cameras once a second.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// What neither mode touches is the zoom. That is the rider's, always: following is a promise
	/// about the centre of the screen, and taking somebody's zoom away once a second would make
	/// the map unusable.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// Every unheld axis is read back from the last reported viewport rather than from
	/// <see cref="_camera"/>, so a rider who has panned while only the bearing is being driven —
	/// or turned the map while only the centre is — keeps what they did.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private async Task AimCameraAsync()
	{
		(double Latitude, double Longitude)? centre = _followMe ? MyPoint : null;
		double? bearing = _headingUp ? MyHeadingDeg : null;

		// Neither mode has anything to say — either both are off, or they are on and waiting for a
		// fix, which the menu states rather than treating as a fault.
		if (centre is null && bearing is null)
		{
			return;
		}

		// A rider stopped at a light still publishes about once a second. Re-sending a camera the
		// map is already at would be an interop call and a full overlay repaint per fix, for a map
		// that is exactly where it should be.
		bool moved = centre is { } point && _followedTo != point;
		bool turned = bearing is { } heading
			&& (_aimedBearing is not { } aimed || BearingDelta(aimed, heading) >= HeadingChangeDeg);

		if (!moved && !turned)
		{
			return;
		}

		if (centre is { } held)
		{
			_followedTo = held;
		}

		if (bearing is { } aimedAt)
		{
			_aimedBearing = aimedAt;
		}

		// Through the component only — _camera is deliberately left where it was. It is the
		// camera the map *opens* on, and a following map re-aims about once a second: writing
		// each of those into the parameter would have every render re-issue the move the
		// component had already made, a second interop call a tick for nothing.
		await ApplyCameraAsync(new MapCamera(
			centre?.Latitude ?? _mapViewport?.CentreLatitude ?? _camera.Latitude,
			centre?.Longitude ?? _mapViewport?.CentreLongitude ?? _camera.Longitude,
			_mapViewport?.ZoomLevel ?? _camera.ZoomLevel,
			bearing ?? _mapViewport?.HeadingDeg ?? _camera.HeadingDeg));
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// The angle between two bearings, 0–180°. Compass degrees wrap, so the plain difference calls
	/// a swing from 359° to 1° a 358° turn — which would be a whole extra rotation of the map every
	/// time a rider drifted across north.
	/// </summary>
	/// <param name="from">Degrees clockwise from north.</param>
	/// <param name="to">Degrees clockwise from north.</param>
	private static double BearingDelta(double from, double to)
	{
		double difference = Math.Abs(to - from) % 360;
		return difference > 180 ? 360 - difference : difference;
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Reads back the view this map was last left on and opens there.
	/// <para>
	/// The camera only applies to the ride it was stored for; <see cref="LiveMapView.FollowMe"/>
	/// and <see cref="LiveMapView.HeadingUp"/> carry across rides, because they describe how this
	/// rider likes to be ridden with rather than one ride's ground.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// Both halves of the handover are covered: assigning <see cref="_camera"/> is what a base map
	/// that has not attached yet picks up at init, and <see cref="ApplyCameraAsync"/> is what moves
	/// one that already has. Which of the two wins the race is a matter of whether the ride's
	/// snapshot or the device store answered first, and neither order should cost the rider their
	/// view.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private async Task RestoreViewAsync()
	{
		LiveMapView? stored = LiveMapView.Decode(await Settings.GetAsync(LiveMapView.StorageKey));

		if (stored is null)
		{
			return;
		}

		_savedView = stored;
		_followMe = stored.FollowMe;
		_headingUp = stored.HeadingUp;

		// Carried across rides like the two above it. A device that has never chosen reads back as
		// on — see LiveMapView.Neighbours — so this is only ever a rider's own "no" arriving.
		_showNeighbours = stored.Neighbours;
		RebuildNeighbours();

		if (stored.IsFor(RideId))
		{
			_camera = stored.ToCamera();
			await ApplyCameraAsync(_camera);
		}

		await InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);

		// After the camera, so a rider who left it following ends up on themselves rather than on
		// the ground they were looking at a ride ago. Silent — no toast: restoring a mode the rider
		// set on a previous screen is not a change of mind, and a map that announced its own
		// settings on every open would be one more thing to dismiss at the start of every ride.
		await AimCameraAsync();
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Holds the current view for the next time this map opens, and lets it through to the device
	/// store at most every <see cref="SaveInterval"/> — or immediately when the caller says so.
	/// </summary>
	/// <param name="now">
	/// True for a deliberate act (the follow toggle, leaving the page, leaving the ride), false for
	/// the pan-by-pan stream that would otherwise write on every frame of a drag.
	/// </param>
	private Task RememberViewAsync(bool now = false)
	{
		// _session.RideId rather than the RideId parameter: on a change of ride the parameter is
		// already the new one while the viewport on screen still belongs to the old.
		if (_session.RideId == Guid.Empty)
		{
			return Task.CompletedTask;
		}

		LiveMapView? view = (_mapViewport is { CanvasWidthPx: > 0 } vp
			? new LiveMapView(_session.RideId, vp.CentreLatitude, vp.CentreLongitude, vp.ZoomLevel, _followMe, _headingUp, _showNeighbours)
			: new LiveMapView(_session.RideId, _camera.Latitude, _camera.Longitude, _camera.ZoomLevel, _followMe, _headingUp, _showNeighbours))
			.Normalised();

		if (view is null || view == _savedView)
		{
			return Task.CompletedTask;
		}

		_pendingView = view;

		DateTimeOffset stamp = Clock.GetUtcNow();
		if (!now && stamp - _lastSavedUtc < SaveInterval)
		{
			// Held, not dropped: DisposeAsync writes whatever is pending on the way out, so the
			// throttle costs a rider at most the last few seconds of panning if the app is killed
			// with the map open.
			return Task.CompletedTask;
		}

		_lastSavedUtc = stamp;
		return SaveViewAsync();
	}

	private async Task SaveViewAsync()
	{
		if (_pendingView is not { } view)
		{
			return;
		}

		_pendingView = null;
		_savedView = view;

		// IDeviceSettings never throws — a browser with site data blocked answers the same as a
		// first run, and a view that cannot be persisted is not worth failing a live map over.
		await Settings.SetAsync(LiveMapView.StorageKey, view.Encode());
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Moves the base map, tolerating one that never attached.
	/// <para>
	/// Driven through the component rather than by assigning <see cref="_camera"/> alone: a
	/// parameter only moves the map when its value differs from the last one applied, so asking
	/// for the same camera twice — following a rider back to a point they had panned away from,
	/// or choosing the same marker again — would do nothing at all the second time.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private async Task ApplyCameraAsync(MapCamera camera)
	{
		if (_map is null)
		{
			return;
		}

		try
		{
			await _map.SetCameraAsync(camera);
		}
		catch
		{
			// A base map that failed to attach shows its own stated error (§4.5). Refusing to
			// move a camera that does not exist is not worth a second error over the top.
		}
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// A tap on the base map either places a marker or asks what is already there (§16.1, §16.4).
	/// Which one it is depends on <see cref="_placingMarker"/>; the "what is here" arithmetic is
	/// <see cref="MarkerHitTest"/>'s, and this decides what to do with the answer.
	/// </summary>
	private async Task OnMapTappedAsync(MapClick click)
	{
		// Placing is answered first, and deliberately ahead of the viewport guard below: the tap
		// arrives carrying its own coordinates, so a map that has not reported a frame yet can
		// still place a marker. It is hit-testing that needs pixels to measure "near" in.
		//
		// It also means an armed tap never opens a pin's detail. Both at once would be a tap that
		// silently did two things, one of which the rider did not ask for.
		if (_placingMarker)
		{
			OpenComposerAt(click);
			return;
		}

		// A tap arriving before the map has reported a frame has nothing to hit-test against.
		if (_mapViewport is not { } viewport)
		{
			return;
		}

		_nearbyMarkerIds = [.. MarkerHitTest.Near(viewport, _session.Markers.Values, click).Select(marker => marker.Id)];

		await InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
	}

	private void CloseNearby() => _nearbyMarkerIds = Array.Empty<Guid>();

	/// <summary>
	/// Expands a row and brings the map to it. Collapsing leaves the camera alone — somebody
	/// closing a panel is done reading, not asking to be taken somewhere.
	/// </summary>
	private async Task ToggleMarkerAsync(MarkerDto marker)
	{
		if (_openMarkerId == marker.Id)
		{
			_openMarkerId = null;
			return;
		}

		_openMarkerId = marker.Id;
		await FocusAsync(marker);
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Centres the map on a marker and zooms in, without zooming <em>out</em> on somebody who
	/// is already closer than <see cref="FocusZoom"/> — pulling the view back would throw away
	/// detail they had chosen.
	/// <para>
	/// Stops following, and says so. Being carried to a marker and then dragged back to yourself a
	/// second later is not a reading of "show me that pin" anybody holds; the rider asked to look
	/// at something, and the mode has to yield to that — but silently dropping a mode is exactly
	/// the surprise <see cref="OnMapGestureAsync"/> exists to avoid, and this drops one for the
	/// same reason and deserves the same sentence.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// Through <see cref="StopFollowing"/> like the other two routes out, so heading-up goes with
	/// it: this puts a marker at the centre of the screen, and a map still turning to the rider's
	/// heading would be rotating about a pin nobody is standing on.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// The bearing itself is left where it is — see the camera below. Choosing a marker says which
	/// ground to look at, not which way up.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private async Task FocusAsync(MarkerDto marker)
	{
		if (_map is null)
		{
			return;
		}

		(bool wasFollowing, bool wasHeadingUp) = StopFollowing();

		if (wasFollowing || wasHeadingUp)
		{
			ShowToast($"{NameStopped(wasFollowing, wasHeadingUp)} — showing you that marker.");
		}

		double zoom = Math.Max(_mapViewport?.ZoomLevel ?? FocusZoom, FocusZoom);

		_camera = new MapCamera(
			PositionScale.ToDegrees(marker.Lat),
			PositionScale.ToDegrees(marker.Lon),
			zoom,
			// Carried rather than defaulted. MapCamera's heading defaults to north, so building one
			// from three arguments is a silent instruction to straighten the map — which on a map
			// the rider has turned, or that "heading up" is turning for them, is a second
			// thing this did that nobody asked for.
			_mapViewport?.HeadingDeg ?? _camera.HeadingDeg);

		await ApplyCameraAsync(_camera);
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Positions + markers merge into one Skia layer: riders draw as a named pill in their own
	/// colour, authored markers as their icon on a plate (§16.3).
	/// </summary>
	private void RebuildMapLayer()
	{
		// The lines arrive already encoded (§15.5), so this is a projection of the session's list
		// rather than any geometry work — the overlay decodes each line once and keeps it.
		// The track id rides along so the overlay can honour a colour the rider has pinned to
		// this particular route on the info page (§18.6). Without it the line would fall back
		// to its position in the list, which is the palette's answer, not theirs.
		_routeLayer = [.. _session.Routes.Select((route, index) =>
			new RouteOverlay(route.EncodedPolyline, RoutePalette.At(index), route.TrackId))];

		_markerLayer.Clear();

		foreach (RiderPositionDto position in _session.Positions.Values)
		{
			if (HasOwnReceiver && position.UserId == Auth.UserId)
			{
				// This rider, as the server has them — dropped on a host that reads its own GPS,
				// and dropped there whether or not the device currently has a fix.
				//
				// It is this phone's own fix after a round trip (§4.2, §5.3), so at best it is a
				// worse copy of what SelfMarker already draws and at worst it is a second mark for
				// one person: at a standstill they sit on top of each other, and at speed they read
				// as two riders a few seconds apart. When there is no device fix — the GPS off or
				// warming up, or the private area suppressing it (§10.1) — the honest answer is
				// that this phone does not currently know where it is, not a point from before it
				// stopped knowing.
				//
				// A browser keeps it, and draws it as an ordinary rider pill: it has no receiver to
				// do better with (§18.6), and what it is showing really is the server's view of
				// this rider — the same one everybody else on the ride is looking at. See
				// HasOwnReceiver.
				continue;
			}

			// The colour and the name both come off the member row, not the fix. A position
			// batch carries neither (§5.3) — the username because it is immutable and would be
			// bytes repeated every tick, the colour for the same reason — so the fix says where
			// somebody is and the member list says who and how they are drawn.
			RideMemberSummary? member = _session.Member(position.UserId);

			_markerLayer[position.UserId] = new MapMarker(
				Id: position.UserId,
				Latitude: PositionScale.ToDegrees(position.Lat),
				Longitude: PositionScale.ToDegrees(position.Lon),
				IconKey: string.Empty,
				Title: string.IsNullOrEmpty(position.UserName) ? member?.UserName ?? "?" : position.UserName,
				DirectionDeg: position.HeadingDeg,
				Kind: MarkerKind.Rider,
				SpeedMps: position.SpeedMps,
				Colour: member?.MarkerColour);
		}
		foreach (MarkerDto marker in _session.Markers.Values)
		{
			_markerLayer[marker.Id] = new MapMarker(
				Id: marker.Id,
				Latitude: PositionScale.ToDegrees(marker.Lat),
				Longitude: PositionScale.ToDegrees(marker.Lon),
				IconKey: marker.Icon,
				Title: marker.Title,
				DirectionDeg: marker.DirectionDeg);
		}

		if (SelfMarker is { } self)
		{
			// Last, so it is added after the authored markers as well as the riders. The overlay
			// draws in dictionary order and this is the mark whose position the rider is reading
			// the map to find.
			_markerLayer[self.Id] = self;
		}

		// Same inputs, a different projection of them: the pins say where everybody is and the panel
		// says how far up the road that is. Here rather than on its own event, because the two things
		// that move a pin are the only two that can move a gap.
		RebuildNeighbours();
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Works out who is immediately ahead and immediately behind this rider on the route (§5.4).
	/// <para>
	/// The whole list is derived first and then narrowed, rather than the ride's positions being
	/// projected here: <see cref="MemberRoster"/> already settles the projection, the three presence
	/// states and the colour every rider is drawn in, and settling any of those a second time is how
	/// this panel and the members screen would start naming different leaders.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// The reader's own place on the route is handed in separately — see <see cref="MyAlongMetres"/>
	/// for why the ride's round-tripped copy of this phone's own fix is not good enough to measure
	/// everybody else against.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private void RebuildNeighbours()
	{
		if (!_showNeighbours || _session.Ride is not { } ride)
		{
			// Nothing on screen to fill, so nothing is spent filling it. Toggling the panel back on
			// rebuilds — see ToggleNeighboursAsync.
			_neighbours = [];
			return;
		}

		_neighbours = NeighbourList.Nearest(
			MemberRoster.Build(
				ride.Members,
				_session.Positions,
				GapRoute,
				MyPoint,
				Auth.UserId,
				Clock.GetUtcNow(),
				MemberSort.AlongRoute),
			Auth.UserId,
			MyAlongMetres);
	}

	// -- Markers ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

	private async Task DeleteMarkerAsync(MarkerDto marker)
	{
		bool ok = await Confirm.AskAsync(
			"Delete marker?",
			$"“{MarkerIconGlyphs.Name(marker.Title, marker.Icon)}” disappears from everyone's map. " +
			"This cannot be undone.",
			confirmText: "Delete",
			danger: true);

		if (!ok) return;

		_deletingMarkerId = marker.Id;
		try
		{
			await _session.DeleteMarkerAsync(marker.Id);

			// Somebody's own delete can land while its detail is open. Collapsing it is the
			// honest response — the alternative is an expanded panel describing a marker that
			// no longer exists.
			if (_openMarkerId == marker.Id)
			{
				_openMarkerId = null;
			}
		}
		finally { _deletingMarkerId = null; }
	}

	// -- Consent ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

	private async Task OnConsentShareAsync()
	{
		_showConsent = false;
		await _session.SetSharingAsync(true);
	}

	private Task OnConsentDismissAsync()
	{
		_showConsent = false;
		return Task.CompletedTask;
	}

	public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
	{
		_session.Changed -= OnSessionChanged;

		// Before anything that can await: its callback renders, and a render after this page has
		// gone is an ObjectDisposedException out of a timer nobody is holding.
		_toastTimer?.Dispose();
		_toastTimer = null;
		if (_privateAreas is { } areas)
		{
			areas.Changed -= OnPrivateAreaChanged;
		}

		RouteStyles.Changed -= OnRouteStyleChanged;

		// The screen goes back to the rider's own timeout. First, because everything below it can
		// await the network — and a page that has left the screen should not be holding the device
		// awake for the length of a hub disconnect on a dead link.
		//
		// Note that this is the map's lock and not the ride's: stepping to the info page or the
		// marker composer gives it up, and the display timeout starts counting again there. Those
		// screens are read with the phone in a hand, at a stop; this one is read at speed with
		// gloves on, which is the case the lock is for.
		await ReleaseScreenAsync();

		// Unsubscribed, not stopped. Leaving this page is not leaving the ride: the receiver keeps
		// running for as long as a ride is being shared with, which is the whole point of it (§4.3).
		if (_broadcast is { } broadcast)
		{
			broadcast.Changed -= OnBroadcastChanged;
		}

		// The write that matters: leaving for the info page, the composer or the thread is the
		// common way off this map, and coming straight back is the common way onto it.
		await RememberViewAsync(now: true);

		await _session.DisposeAsync();
	}
}

<style>
	/* The map is the page — it fills the shell that .page-full pinned to the content area. */
	.live .map-fill {
		position: absolute;
		inset: 0;
	}

	.live .map-fill .dlr-map-container {
		height: 100%;
		min-height: 0;
	}

	/*
		The hamburger and its menu. `position: absolute` inside .page-full rather than fixed,
		so the whole assembly lands inside the content area on both rail placements without
		restating the rail's geometry a second time.
	*/
	.live .chrome {
		position: absolute;
		/* Shared with the base map's compass on the opposite edge, so the two line up (app.css).
		   --dlr-safe-right rather than the raw env(): landscape reports a side inset on both
		   edges, and the raw one would float this button a cutout's width in from a right edge
		   that has no cutout. */
		top: var(--dlr-map-control-top);
		right: max(var(--dlr-map-control-inset), var(--dlr-safe-right));
		z-index: 20;
		/* A column now that following has a button of its own. The menu is absolutely positioned
		   and so takes no part in this. */
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		align-items: flex-end;
		gap: 0.5rem;
	}

	/* One geometry for every button in this stack — and the same 48 px the nav rail and the base
	   map's compass use (app.css). They are read at speed and pressed with gloves on, so they are
	   a matched set rather than three sizes that happen to be similar. */
	.live .chrome > button {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		justify-content: center;
		/* Anchors the "turning" dot below. */
		position: relative;
		width: var(--dlr-map-control-size);
		height: var(--dlr-map-control-size);
		padding: 0;
		background: var(--dlr-accent);
		color: var(--dlr-accent-fg);
		border: 2px solid var(--dlr-accent-fg);
		border-radius: 8px;
		font-size: 20px;
		cursor: pointer;
		box-shadow: 0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.35);
	}

	/*
		The two camera modes — "centre on me and follow", and "turn the map to my heading" — with
		nothing switched on.

		Deliberately quieter than the hamburger: colours reversed, so the surface is the map's
		chrome rather than the accent. Three accent-filled buttons stacked on top of each other
		read as one control split in thirds, and the hamburger is the one that has to be found
		first by a rider who does not yet know this screen. It also leaves the accent meaning one
		thing in this stack — "this mode is on" — rather than two.
	*/
	.live .chrome > button.follow,
	.live .chrome > button.heading-up {
		background: var(--dlr-surface);
		color: var(--dlr-fg);
		border-color: var(--dlr-border-strong);
	}

	/*
		On. The state has to survive a visor in daylight, so it is a filled button and not a tint:
		colour alone is the first thing to go, and these are read at a glance while moving. It is
		also now the only statement either mode makes with the menu closed — the dot that used to
		sit on the hamburger's corner went when the second button arrived, because two indicators
		for one mode is one of them going stale.
	*/
	.live .chrome > button.follow.on,
	.live .chrome > button.heading-up.on {
		background: var(--dlr-accent);
		color: var(--dlr-accent-fg);
		border-color: var(--dlr-accent-fg);
	}

	/* Sits under the hamburger and over the map: a tap that dismisses must not also pan.
	   Absolute inside .page-full, so it stops at the nav rail rather than swallowing it. */
	.live .menu-backdrop {
		position: absolute;
		inset: 0;
		z-index: 19;
	}

	.live .menu {
		position: absolute;
		/* Clear of the whole stack, not just the hamburger it hangs from: a menu that covered a
		   camera mode's button would hide the only statement that mode makes. Three controls and
		   the two gaps between them, expressed in the same tokens the stack is built from so the
		   two cannot drift apart. */
		top: calc(3 * var(--dlr-map-control-size) + 1.5rem);
		right: 0;
		min-width: 15rem;
		padding: 0.35rem 0;
		background: var(--dlr-surface);
		border: 1px solid var(--dlr-border);
		border-radius: 8px;
		box-shadow: 0 8px 24px rgba(0,0,0,0.45);
	}

	.live .menu button {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		gap: 0.6rem;
		width: 100%;
		padding: 0.6rem 1rem;
		background: transparent;
		border: 0;
		color: var(--dlr-fg);
		font: inherit;
		text-align: left;
		white-space: nowrap;
		cursor: pointer;
	}

	.live .menu button:hover {
		background: var(--dlr-accent-soft);
	}

	/* A mode that is on reads as on with the menu open too, not only from the dot outside it. */
	.live .menu button.checked {
		background: var(--dlr-accent-soft);
	}

	.live .menu button.checked i {
		color: var(--dlr-accent);
	}

	.live .menu button i {
		color: var(--dlr-fg-muted);
	}

	.live .menu .count, .live .menu .quiet {
		margin-left: auto;
		font-size: 0.8rem;
		color: var(--dlr-fg-muted);
	}

	/* The receiver's state. Reads as a caption under the menu's items rather than as another
	   one of them — it is the only row that is not a control, and a row that looks tappable
	   and is not is worse than one that is plainly a label. */
	.live .menu .gps {
		display: flex;
		align-items: flex-start;
		gap: 0.6rem;
		padding: 0.5rem 1rem 0.35rem;
		margin-top: 0.35rem;
		border-top: 1px solid var(--dlr-border);
		font-size: 0.8rem;
		color: var(--dlr-fg-muted);
		/* The sentence wraps; the items above it deliberately do not. */
		white-space: normal;
		max-width: 18rem;
	}

	.live .menu .gps i {
		margin-top: 0.15rem;
	}

	/* Sits where the map's own error strip does, and never at the same time as one — that one
	   is raised by a menu action and this one by the receiver, and the rider is not doing both. */
	.live .gps-alert {
		top: max(0.75rem, env(safe-area-inset-top));
		color: red;
	}

	/*
		"Offline — this ride as it was 12 min ago."

		Shares the .map-error slot with the two above it, and is deliberately not styled as an
		error: nothing has failed in a way the rider can act on, and a red strip that cannot be
		dismissed and cannot be fixed at the side of a road becomes something to ignore. It is a
		plain surface chip like the placing strip at the other end of the map — legible over
		whatever the tiles are doing, and stating a fact rather than raising an alarm.

		It carries its own box because .map-error is only geometry; the red one gets its
		background from .error in app.css, and this one is not that.
	*/
	.live .map-error.cached {
		top: max(0.75rem, env(safe-area-inset-top));
		padding: 0.4rem 0.8rem;
		background: var(--dlr-surface);
		color: var(--dlr-fg);
		border: 1px solid var(--dlr-border);
		border-radius: 8px;
		box-shadow: 0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.35);
		font-size: 0.85rem;
	}

	.live .map-error.cached i {
		color: var(--dlr-fg-muted);
	}

	.live .menu hr {
		margin: 0.35rem 0;
		border: 0;
		border-top: 1px solid var(--dlr-border);
	}

	/*
		"Tap the map where the marker goes."

		Along the BOTTOM, not the top: the top edge already carries the compass on one side and
		the hamburger on the other, and a strip threaded between them on a phone has about
		270 px to say anything in. The bottom is empty, it is where the thumb already is for
		the Cancel button, and it leaves the ground the rider is aiming at unobscured.

		Inside .page-full, which on a phone already stops above the nav bar — the safe-area
		term is what keeps it off the home indicator in landscape, where the map does run to
		the bottom of the screen.
	*/
	.live .placing {
		position: absolute;
		left: 50%;
		transform: translateX(-50%);
		bottom: max(0.75rem, env(safe-area-inset-bottom));
		z-index: 18;
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		gap: 0.75rem;
		max-width: min(28rem, calc(100% - 1.5rem));
		padding: 0.5rem 0.5rem 0.5rem 1rem;
		background: var(--dlr-surface);
		color: var(--dlr-fg);
		border: 1px solid var(--dlr-border);
		border-radius: 8px;
		box-shadow: 0 8px 24px rgba(0,0,0,0.45);
	}

	.live .placing button {
		flex-shrink: 0;
		/* The same 48 px tap target the hamburger and the rail use — this one is pressed with
		   gloves on, at the side of a road, to get out of a mode. */
		min-height: 48px;
		padding: 0 0.9rem;
		background: transparent;
		border: 1px solid var(--dlr-border-strong);
		border-radius: 6px;
		color: var(--dlr-fg);
		font: inherit;
		cursor: pointer;
	}

	.live .placing button:hover {
		background: var(--dlr-accent-soft);
	}

	/*
		"Following you — the map stays centred as you ride."

		Along the bottom for the same reasons the placing strip is: the top edge is spoken for by
		the compass on one side and the control stack on the other, and what is left between them
		on a phone is about 270 px. The bottom is empty, and it leaves the middle of the map — the
		part a rider being followed is looking at — clear.

